# Situation

Repo: claude-engineering-skills (public upstream). Its sync script (`scripts/sync-to-repos.mjs`) copies a tooling bundle into consumer repos. Per consumer it keeps a gitignored `scripts/.sync-manifest.json` (`files: { <consumer path>: "sha256:…" }`) and a committed, append-only `.sync-receipt.json`; a consumer can also declare standing overrides in `.sync-overrides.json`. A "refused" path is one the sync declined to overwrite because the consumer had changed it (diverged from the recorded base); a "held" path is one covered by a declared override. A separate verifier, `sync-isolation-verify.mjs`, runs 8 gates against a consumer checkout.

Base is `main@d0f3d8f5` (2026-11-14). You may assume you can run the real sync CLI (`node scripts/sync-to-repos.mjs --target-path <repo>`) against a freshly created disposable consumer git repo and inspect its manifest, disk and output across several consecutive syncs.

# The report

The coordinator of `storyline` (a consumer repo) sent this to the upstream coordinator:

> The sync writes the hash of what it **intended** to write into `scripts/.sync-manifest.json`, including for paths it refused as diverged — so each refusal re-asserts a false base, the frozen set can only grow, and `verify-sync-invariants` cannot see it because it only checks that the manifest exists and parses.
>
> Evidence: `.vscode/mcp.json` is `29ec5697` in the manifest and `d884e509` on disk, for a path the sync refused. Read as "the manifest holds upstream's intent". Also: 8/8 gates are green while 11 paths disagree between manifest and disk.

A peer who read the report summarised it as a "ratchet": each refusal moves the recorded base forward without writing the file.

